  "notes": "The heavy-808 end of the ringtone era: the fastest half-time band in this batch (74–86 felt, stored as its 148–172 straight equivalent), a very large 808 kick, and hats deliberately thinner than Atlanta's so the low end owns the record. Two things separate it from the snap bench next to it. First it has real harmonic content — 4-bar i–VI–VII loops and looped soul progressions rather than a two-note figure — so `maxChords` is 4 and `chordDurationBeats` runs long. Second the marching / brass-band snare figures are authored as a tom-and-crash perc layer with the highest perc density here, which is what those figures are once they leave the snare. The 808's register is pushed low and narrow because the research's reason for it is mix placement: \"tuned low with long decay, placed to leave the sample's own bass audible\". ⚠ `low` is the research's own verdict: it says outright that credits and era are documented, that **no technical interview was locatable**, and that the parameters are marked inferences. The drum placements, the snare-roll build, the hat density, the perc lanes and both tempo bands are all INFERRED, and the paragraph marks the drums row and both BPM bands LOW-CONF specifically. The 96–110 straight pop band is NOT modelled — this entry commits to the rap lane. ⚠ `fills.snareRollLadder` is off: with both fill periods at 4 bars the big fill wins and the inherited ladder reads no seed, so it would write one identical fill on every one of 200 — and the snare-roll build into the hook is exactly what `snareRoll` is describing instead. ⚠ `anticipationProb` raised to 0.15. With `mirror_kick` against a fully locked 808 both parts take every kick, so every bass onset lands exactly on an 808 onset — `coherence.rs` calls that one line played twice rather than two parts locking. The lock is the style and is untouched; the anticipation is what makes them two parts.",
